Osteoporotic Fractures (e.g. Neck of Femur)
Geriatric & Frailty
Low-impact fragility fracture, Shortened/externally rotated leg (femur), Sudden localized bone pain, DXA T-score <= -2.5

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-031
Reason for Visit: Acute severe back and groin pain following a low-impact fall, rendering the patient completely immobile.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient with a high risk of osteoporosis who sustained a fall from standing height and is now unable to bear weight due to agonizing groin and back pain. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out major long-bone fractures (neck of femur) or acute vertebral compression fractures, provide immediate analgesia, and coordinate safe emergency transport prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I slipped in the hallway, and now my hip and back are completely locked in agonizing pain; I can't even sit up."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden onset of severe, localized bone pain in the right groin and mid-thoracic back following a low-impact fall from standing height approximately three hours ago. They report an absolute inability to bear weight on the affected limb immediately following the injury, requiring the spouse to provide pillows while the patient remains on the floor. The spouse notes an established clinical history of severely low bone mineral density (DXA T-score <= -2.5, matching the anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding) and prior fragility fractures (Colles' fracture of the distal radius commonly resulting from a fall onto an outstretched hand five years ago). The patient also has a progressive development of severe kyphosis or a dowager's hump over time with a loss of overall standing height. They complain of acute severe back pain indicating a likely new vertebral compression fracture.
Functional Status: Completely immobile on the floor; baseline independent with a frame.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of hypothermia and pressure injury on the floor; surgical emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 158/92 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Visibly pale, diaphoretic, in severe pain, lying supine on the hallway floor.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals the classic presentation of a shortened and externally rotated lower right limb. There is exquisite localized bony tenderness over the right greater trochanter, and pain is severely exacerbated by passive microscopic internal rotation of the hip. Examination of the spine (via careful log-roll technique) reveals localized exquisite bony tenderness over the T10-T12 spinous processes with associated paraspinal muscle spasm. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis) are palpable and intact. (A low-impact fragility fracture with a shortened/externally rotated leg and sudden localized bone pain are confirmed tetrad findings; DXA T-score is confirmed by history).
Environmental Observations: A loose rug is present at the site of the fall. The house is cold.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular calcium/vitamin D but ceased bisphosphonates two years ago due to dental issues.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Osteoporotic Fractures - Fractured Right Neck of Femur and Acute Thoracic Vertebral Compression Fracture - Status: Acute Orthopaedic Emergency
Differentials:

Pelvic Ring Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound shortening and external rotation of the leg, which are the hallmark physical signs of a proximal femur fracture.

Hip Dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Low-energy mechanism of injury and the external rotation of the leg (posterior dislocations typically present internally rotated).

Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy (Sciatica) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of acute bony point tenderness, the mechanism of trauma, and the gross anatomical deformity of the limb.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-threatening orthopaedic trauma)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic textbook signs of a fractured neck of femur (shortened, externally rotated leg, inability to weight-bear) following a minor mechanical fall, set against a background of severe established osteoporosis. Telephone triage accurately identified a major trauma scenario in a frail adult. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have been catastrophic, risking severe hypovolemic shock, prolonged agonizing pain, and severe pressure necrosis from a long lie. This urgent after-hours assessment allowed for the clinical confirmation of the gross anatomical deformity, the provision of immediate potent intramuscular analgesia, and the rapid coordination of emergency ambulance transport directly to an orthopaedic surgical centre.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate strong intramuscular analgesia (e.g., morphine 5mg IM) to manage the agonizing pain while awaiting ambulance transport. Provided a detailed clinical handover to the responding paramedic team, specifically highlighting the suspected concurrent spinal injury to ensure strict spinal precautions during extrication.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Not applicable in an acute trauma scenario requiring immediate transfer; focus is entirely on stabilizing the patient).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Kept the patient warm with blankets to prevent hypothermia; strictly instructed the spouse not to attempt to move the patient.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department Orthopaedic team.

